<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    Sipatokkong — Buginese/Makassarese for "mutually supporting", which is also the shape of the
    app: a volunteer sets the work, a parent carries it out, the parent reports back.

    Two rounded forms propping each other up. The pale teal one is longer, thicker and more
    upright — it is doing the supporting; the orange one is shorter and leans onto it. The legs are
    deliberately unequal: a symmetric tent reads as a roof or a chevron, and only an asymmetric one
    reads as support.

    The supporting form is drawn SECOND so it overlaps the join. That z-order is the whole idea —
    overlapping says "holding up", merely touching says "next to".

    Drawn as round-capped strokes rather than filled outlines: the caps *are* the rounded ends, and
    each form stays one line of pathData that can be nudged without redrawing an outline.

    Everything sits inside the 66dp centred safe zone (21..87 of the 108 viewport), so no mask can
    clip it: the hull is x 27..79, y 25..84, optically centred on (54, 54).
-->
<vector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    android:width="108dp"
    android:height="108dp"
    android:viewportWidth="108"
    android:viewportHeight="108">

    <!-- supported form -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M72,77 Q64,60 57,47"
        android:fillColor="#00000000"
        android:strokeColor="#F8991D"
        android:strokeWidth="14"
        android:strokeLineCap="round" />

    <!-- supporting form, drawn over the join -->
    <path
        android:pathData="M35,76 Q42,52 55,33"
        android:fillColor="#00000000"
        android:strokeColor="#CDE7E0"
        android:strokeWidth="16"
        android:strokeLineCap="round" />
</vector>
